# Case 01: "I was charged twice after upgrading"

**Severity:** Medium | **Category:** Invoicing / Proration

## Customer-reported symptom
"I upgraded from Basic to Pro yesterday and now I see two charges
on my card statement. Please refund one."

## Investigation steps
1. Find the customer and open their subscription timeline.
2. List all invoices from the last 7 days.
3. Compare invoice line items against the card statement amounts.
4. Check the date of the plan change and the billing cycle dates.
5. Check whether any payment failed and was retried.

## What I found (example)
- Invoice A: renewal charge for Basic, created before the upgrade
- Invoice B: upgrade invoice with two lines:
  - Credit for unused time on Basic (negative)
  - Prorated charge for Pro (positive)
- No duplicate payments. Two invoices exist, each paid once.

## Root cause
The customer's renewal for Basic and the upgrade fell within the
same period. The upgrade created a separate prorated invoice, so
two legitimate charges appeared. No billing error occurred.

## Fix
No refund needed. If a real duplicate payment had been found
(same invoice paid twice), I would refund the duplicate and
document the cause.

## Reply to customer
Hi [Name],

Thanks for flagging this, and I understand why two charges looked
concerning. I checked your account and both charges are correct:

1. Your regular Basic renewal on [date]: $X
2. Your upgrade to Pro on [date]: $Y. This covers the Pro plan for
   the rest of this cycle, minus a credit for the unused Basic time.

No payment was duplicated, and your next invoice will be a single
charge of $Z on [date]. I've attached both invoices with the line
items. If anything still looks off, just reply and I'll take
another look.

## When to escalate
- The same invoice shows two successful payments
- Proration amounts don't match the documented calculation
- The customer's subscription state doesn't match their invoices

## Escalation note (if needed)
Include: customer ID, invoice IDs, timestamps, expected vs. actual
amounts, and screenshots of the timeline.

## What I learned
Most "double charge" reports are two valid invoices. The skill is
proving it with the line items, then explaining it simply.
